WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.081 --> 00:00:02.949
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:03.193 --> 00:00:07.523
In our previous lesson, we explored the arrangement and movement of particles

3
00:00:07.639 --> 00:00:09.880
in solids, liquids and gases.

4
00:00:10.275 --> 00:00:13.909
Today, we are going to build on that knowledge by examining how

5
00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:16.533
substances change from one state to another.

6
00:00:16.928 --> 00:00:20.585
We will explain melting, freezing, boiling, evaporation

7
00:00:20.655 --> 00:00:25.055
and condensation, and explore the role of energy in these processes.

8
00:00:25.391 --> 00:00:27.586
Let us begin with a familiar example.

9
00:00:27.864 --> 00:00:31.150
Imagine taking an ice cube from the freezer and placing it in

10
00:00:31.196 --> 00:00:31.893
a saucepan.

11
00:00:32.287 --> 00:00:36.014
As the ice absorbs energy from its surroundings, its temperature

12
00:00:36.061 --> 00:00:38.777
increases until it reaches its melting point.

13
00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:42.864
For pure water at normal atmospheric pressure, this is zero

14
00:00:42.945 --> 00:00:43.979
degrees Celsius.

15
00:00:44.431 --> 00:00:48.065
At the melting point, the ice begins to change into liquid water.

16
00:00:48.402 --> 00:00:51.025
But what exactly happens to its particles?

17
00:00:51.234 --> 00:00:54.764
In a solid, particles vibrate about fixed positions.

18
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.746
As energy is transferred to the solid, the particles gain

19
00:00:58.839 --> 00:01:01.021
energy and vibrate more vigorously.

20
00:01:01.474 --> 00:01:05.352
At the melting point, the energy being supplied allows the particles

21
00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:09.160
to overcome enough of the forces holding them in fixed positions

22
00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:10.809
to move past one another.

23
00:01:11.424 --> 00:01:13.816
The solid therefore becomes a liquid.

24
00:01:13.932 --> 00:01:15.975
This process is called melting.

25
00:01:16.427 --> 00:01:20.352
An important point to remember is that during melting, the temperature

26
00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:24.345
of a pure substance remains constant until the entire solid

27
00:01:24.438 --> 00:01:27.805
has melted, provided the pressure remains constant.

28
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.171
This is because the energy supplied is being used to overcome forces

29
00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:36.269
of attraction between particles rather than increasing their average

30
00:01:36.316 --> 00:01:37.372
kinetic energy.

31
00:01:37.523 --> 00:01:40.414
Now, let us continue heating the liquid water.

32
00:01:40.623 --> 00:01:44.466
As energy is transferred to the water, the particles gain kinetic

33
00:01:44.536 --> 00:01:46.068
energy and move faster.

34
00:01:46.312 --> 00:01:49.017
Eventually, the water reaches its boiling point.

35
00:01:49.412 --> 00:01:53.023
At normal atmospheric pressure, pure water boils at one

36
00:01:53.069 --> 00:01:54.590
hundred degrees Celsius.

37
00:01:54.927 --> 00:01:58.793
During boiling, bubbles of water vapour form throughout the liquid

38
00:01:58.851 --> 00:02:00.267
and rise to the surface.

39
00:02:00.476 --> 00:02:04.238
The energy supplied enables the water molecules to overcome

40
00:02:04.273 --> 00:02:07.663
the attractive forces that keep them close together in the liquid.

41
00:02:07.941 --> 00:02:09.927
The water changes into a gas.

42
00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:13.862
Again, during boiling, the temperature remains constant until the

43
00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.312
liquid has completely changed into gas.

44
00:02:16.591 --> 00:02:20.282
Now, you may be wondering: what is the difference between boiling

45
00:02:20.364 --> 00:02:21.513
and evaporation?

46
00:02:21.757 --> 00:02:25.670
Both processes involve a liquid changing into a gas,

47
00:02:25.786 --> 00:02:27.330
but they occur differently.

48
00:02:27.539 --> 00:02:31.301
Evaporation occurs at the surface of a liquid and can happen at

49
00:02:31.347 --> 00:02:33.437
temperatures below its boiling point.

50
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.628
For example, wet clothes can dry outside even when the temperature

51
00:02:37.675 --> 00:02:39.706
is only fifteen degrees Celsius.

52
00:02:40.043 --> 00:02:43.689
This happens because some particles at the surface have enough energy to

53
00:02:43.782 --> 00:02:45.930
escape from the liquid into the air.

54
00:02:46.324 --> 00:02:50.040
Boiling, on the other hand, occurs throughout the liquid at its boiling

55
00:02:50.121 --> 00:02:50.609
point.

56
00:02:50.818 --> 00:02:52.698
Now, let us reverse the process.

57
00:02:52.942 --> 00:02:57.075
When water vapour loses energy, its particles move more slowly.

58
00:02:57.412 --> 00:03:01.452
As they come closer together, attractive forces cause the gas to change

59
00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:02.393
into a liquid.

60
00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.156
This process is called condensation.

61
00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:09.417
You can observe condensation when water droplets form on the outside

62
00:03:09.452 --> 00:03:10.462
of a cold glass.

63
00:03:10.799 --> 00:03:14.456
Water vapour in the surrounding air loses energy when it comes

64
00:03:14.514 --> 00:03:18.589
into contact with the cold surface and condenses into liquid water.

65
00:03:18.984 --> 00:03:22.664
If liquid water continues to lose energy, it eventually reaches

66
00:03:22.734 --> 00:03:23.720
its freezing point.

67
00:03:23.999 --> 00:03:27.854
At zero degrees Celsius, under normal atmospheric

68
00:03:27.923 --> 00:03:30.547
pressure, pure water begins to freeze.

69
00:03:30.884 --> 00:03:34.645
During freezing, particles lose energy, and attractive

70
00:03:34.704 --> 00:03:38.802
forces hold them in fixed positions within the solid structure.

71
00:03:38.976 --> 00:03:42.586
The particles continue vibrating, but they can no longer

72
00:03:42.668 --> 00:03:44.560
move freely past one another.

73
00:03:44.769 --> 00:03:48.763
Let us now consider another change of state called sublimation.

74
00:03:49.007 --> 00:03:52.826
Sublimation occurs when a solid changes directly into

75
00:03:52.919 --> 00:03:55.671
a gas without passing through the liquid state.

76
00:03:55.912 --> 00:03:59.569
An example is dry ice, which is solid carbon dioxide.

77
00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:03.644
At normal atmospheric pressure, it changes directly into carbon

78
00:04:03.702 --> 00:04:04.805
dioxide gas.

79
00:04:05.258 --> 00:04:09.403
The reverse process, in which a gas changes directly into a solid,

80
00:04:09.646 --> 00:04:11.040
is called deposition.

81
00:04:11.191 --> 00:04:14.720
Here is an important G C S E Chemistry principle.

82
00:04:15.173 --> 00:04:19.109
Changes of state are physical changes, not chemical reactions.

83
00:04:19.445 --> 00:04:23.230
When ice melts into water, no new substance is formed.

84
00:04:23.509 --> 00:04:26.040
The water molecules remain H two O.

85
00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:29.639
Only their arrangement, movement and separation change.

86
00:04:29.882 --> 00:04:32.437
These changes are also reversible.

87
00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.047
Now, let us work through an examination-style example.

88
00:04:36.291 --> 00:04:40.064
A student heats a sample of ice from minus ten degrees

89
00:04:40.134 --> 00:04:44.023
Celsius until it becomes steam at one hundred degrees

90
00:04:44.081 --> 00:04:44.836
Celsius.

91
00:04:45.289 --> 00:04:49.097
Initially, the temperature of the ice rises as the particles gain

92
00:04:49.166 --> 00:04:50.234
kinetic energy.

93
00:04:50.687 --> 00:04:54.437
At zero degrees Celsius, the temperature remains constant

94
00:04:54.507 --> 00:04:55.819
while the ice melts.

95
00:04:56.434 --> 00:05:00.150
Once all the ice has melted, the temperature of the liquid water

96
00:05:00.231 --> 00:05:00.835
rises.

97
00:05:01.171 --> 00:05:05.258
At one hundred degrees Celsius, the temperature remains constant

98
00:05:05.316 --> 00:05:07.092
again while the water boils.

99
00:05:07.429 --> 00:05:11.260
This pattern produces a heating curve, which is an important

100
00:05:11.318 --> 00:05:14.163
graph in G C S E Chemistry.

101
00:05:14.615 --> 00:05:16.461
Let us test your understanding.

102
00:05:16.856 --> 00:05:20.014
Which change of state occurs when a gas becomes a liquid?

103
00:05:20.223 --> 00:05:21.175
Condensation.

104
00:05:21.419 --> 00:05:24.542
Which process occurs when a liquid changes into a solid?

105
00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:25.912
Freezing.

106
00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:30.033
And which process allows wet clothes to dry below the boiling point

107
00:05:30.091 --> 00:05:30.730
of water?

108
00:05:30.904 --> 00:05:31.856
Evaporation.

109
00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:35.641
Now, here is a G C S E examination question.

110
00:05:35.815 --> 00:05:39.298
Explain why energy must be supplied when water boils.

111
00:05:39.576 --> 00:05:41.678
This question is worth three marks.

112
00:05:41.852 --> 00:05:45.521
A model answer would be: Water molecules are held close

113
00:05:45.556 --> 00:05:47.518
together by attractive forces.

114
00:05:47.971 --> 00:05:51.709
Energy must be supplied to overcome these forces so

115
00:05:51.779 --> 00:05:55.564
that the molecules can move farther apart and form a gas.

116
00:05:56.016 --> 00:05:59.813
During boiling, the temperature remains constant because

117
00:05:59.848 --> 00:06:03.922
the energy supplied is used to separate the molecules rather

118
00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:06.929
than increase their average kinetic energy.

119
00:06:07.382 --> 00:06:11.353
Remember, the chemical bonds within the water molecules are not broken

120
00:06:11.422 --> 00:06:12.386
during boiling.

121
00:06:12.781 --> 00:06:16.565
It is the forces between the molecules that are overcome.

122
00:06:16.902 --> 00:06:20.536
If you found this lesson useful, please subscribe to J

123
00:06:20.629 --> 00:06:24.553
D Science and visit j d science dot co dot

124
00:06:24.646 --> 00:06:28.419
u k for more detailed G C S E Chemistry

125
00:06:28.477 --> 00:06:32.842
explanations, worked examples and examination questions.

126
00:06:33.237 --> 00:06:36.244
Thank you for watching, and I will see you in the next

127
00:06:36.302 --> 00:06:36.790
lesson.
